WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.709
One last visit to the theatre.

2
00:00:04.709 --> 00:00:08.259
A touring show uses the same prompt book every night.

3
00:00:08.259 --> 00:00:11.808
Say the stage manager wants every lighting cue timed.

4
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:18.298
Instead, the stage manager slips a note into the prompt book before rehearsal: start a stopwatch here.

5
00:00:18.298 --> 00:00:23.887
And in an emergency, they can walk in mid-performance and paste a correction over one page.

6
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.440
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.376
Can you really change the code of a program that's already running, without a restart?

8
00:00:32.376 --> 00:00:35.188
And if you can, can you change anything you like?

9
00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:39.296
Lessons 4 and 5 added work by wrapping objects.

10
00:00:39.296 --> 00:00:42.712
That needs an interface, or a class that isn't final.

11
00:00:42.712 --> 00:00:45.612
It misses self calls when the proxy forwards.

12
00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:49.157
And it only works on objects created through the proxy.

13
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:53.829
A Java agent works on the class instead of the object.

14
00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:57.312
It's an ordinary class with a special entry point.

15
00:00:57.312 --> 00:01:03.443
The JVM hands it an Instrumentation, the JDK's handle for watching and changing classes.

16
00:01:03.443 --> 00:01:05.603
That's the stage manager's key.

17
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:13.293
The simplest way in is a flag: java -javaagent, then the agent's jar and its arguments.

18
00:01:13.293 --> 00:01:17.256
The JVM calls the agent's premain before your main.

19
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:22.797
The first thing most agents do is register a ClassFileTransformer.

20
00:01:22.797 --> 00:01:32.410
From then on, the JVM calls its transform method with the bytes of every class it is about to define: the class files of the last lesson.

21
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:36.619
transform returns new bytes, or null, which means no change.

22
00:01:36.619 --> 00:01:39.847
Our demo agent only watches, and returns null.

23
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:45.564
The demo runs a small program with this agent, and the argument opening-night.

24
00:01:45.564 --> 00:01:48.701
It prints main started, then makes a Ticket record.

25
00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:51.223
When does the agent see the Ticket class?

26
00:01:51.223 --> 00:01:52.207
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.393
Here's what it printed on JDK 25.

28
00:01:58.393 --> 00:02:03.209
premain ran first, when 890 JDK classes had already loaded.

29
00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:07.453
Then the agent saw AgentTarget, before main started.

30
00:02:07.453 --> 00:02:09.249
And the Ticket record?

31
00:02:09.249 --> 00:02:11.371
Just before its first use.

32
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:19.218
So 890 classes were seated before the agent even said hello: the JDK always gets to rehearsal early.

33
00:02:19.218 --> 00:02:26.227
But your own classes load after premain returns, so a startup agent sees almost all of them.

34
00:02:26.833 --> 00:02:31.000
That's lesson 1's props, fetched only when a scene first needs them.

35
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:36.829
The rule: a transformer sees each class once, as the JVM defines it.

36
00:02:36.829 --> 00:02:40.501
Register it before the classes you care about load.

37
00:02:40.733 --> 00:02:43.492
But what about a class that loaded an hour ago?

38
00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:46.982
For that, there's retransformClasses.

39
00:02:46.982 --> 00:02:53.119
It runs the registered transformers again on loaded classes, and installs the result in place.

40
00:02:53.119 --> 00:02:57.493
The Class object stays the same, so every object sees the new code.

41
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.515
The second demo has a final class, StageClock, whose now returns tick.

42
00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:09.507
It creates a clock, attaches an agent to its own JVM, and adds timing code to now.

43
00:03:09.733 --> 00:03:12.885
The clock was created before the agent existed.

44
00:03:12.885 --> 00:03:17.043
On its next call, which body runs, the old one or the new one?

45
00:03:17.043 --> 00:03:18.116
Pause and guess.

46
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.864
The new one.

47
00:03:22.864 --> 00:03:24.232
Before: tick.

48
00:03:24.232 --> 00:03:30.655
Then, from inside now: StageClock.now took 14176 nanoseconds.

49
00:03:30.655 --> 00:03:31.919
After: tick.

50
00:03:31.919 --> 00:03:35.393
The same object, with no restart.

51
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.781
It still says tick.

52
00:03:36.781 --> 00:03:39.515
It just has someone holding a stopwatch now.

53
00:03:39.515 --> 00:03:44.175
And its class is final, so no subclass from lesson 5 could have touched it.

54
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.754
The JDK's documentation adds two things.

55
00:03:47.754 --> 00:03:53.261
A call that's already running finishes on the old code; new calls get the new code.

56
00:03:53.261 --> 00:04:01.355
And the initializer doesn't run again, so static fields keep their values: lesson 1's dress rehearsal is never held twice.

57
00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:04.056
There's also a hard limit.

58
00:04:04.056 --> 00:04:12.385
Retransformation must not add, remove or rename fields or methods, change signatures or modifiers, or change inheritance.

59
00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:14.587
You can rewrite what a cue says.

60
00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:17.547
You can't add a character to the cast list.

61
00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:20.876
Here the prompt book picture breaks.

62
00:04:20.876 --> 00:04:24.155
A pasted correction changes one copy of the book.

63
00:04:24.155 --> 00:04:31.649
But a class exists once per class loader, so one retransformation changes every object and every caller at once.

64
00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:36.670
Retransformation swaps method bodies in place, for every caller at once.

65
00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.005
It never changes the class's shape.

66
00:04:39.933 --> 00:04:40.980
One more thing.

67
00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:47.750
That demo also printed four warning lines, because its agent arrived late, while the program ran.

68
00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:52.775
The flag EnableDynamicAgentLoading, with a plus, opts in and hides them.

69
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:54.664
So, the names.

70
00:04:54.664 --> 00:04:59.276
The stage manager is the agent, and the key is the Instrumentation.

71
00:04:59.276 --> 00:05:03.613
The note slipped in before rehearsal is a ClassFileTransformer.

72
00:05:03.613 --> 00:05:07.124
The correction pasted mid-show is retransformation.

73
00:05:08.033 --> 00:05:09.103
Rules of thumb.

74
00:05:09.103 --> 00:05:13.381
To see a class as it loads, register early, with -javaagent.

75
00:05:13.381 --> 00:05:15.591
Change bodies, never the shape.

76
00:05:15.591 --> 00:05:20.796
And if an agent arrives late, expect the warning, and decide on the flag.

77
00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.314
So, can you change a running program?

78
00:05:24.314 --> 00:05:29.542
Its method bodies, yes, with no restart, under objects that already exist.

79
00:05:29.542 --> 00:05:30.531
Its shape, no.

80
00:05:31.433 --> 00:05:32.365
Quick check.

81
00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:38.657
To count calls to StageClock.now, your retransformation adds a field, long calls.

82
00:05:38.657 --> 00:05:39.667
What happens?

83
00:05:42.833 --> 00:05:43.454
It can't.

84
00:05:43.454 --> 00:05:46.005
Retransformation must not add fields.

85
00:05:46.005 --> 00:05:51.728
The count must live somewhere else, such as a public class that the new code calls.
